speaker Les Brown.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.550
She also has been involved

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.740
in several different interviews,

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.590
has appeared on several platforms

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:40.840
in the National Media Space,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.860
on talk shows,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.990
on the Audible Original, We've Got Answers

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.300
and on radio shows like
The Breakfast Club.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.540
she's also been recruited

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.200
by different fortune 500 companies

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.760
to actually talk about
leadership development

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.140
and the importance of incorporating

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.350
strong and the relevant mental health care

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.870
in those conversations.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.690
So again, grateful to welcome,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.490
Attorney Corey Minor
Smith to the Addy Hour.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.323
- Thank you for having me,

00:03:01.323 --> 00:03:03.670
it's my pleasure to join you today.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.420
- Pleasure having both of you here.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.380
And obviously there's
so much important work

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.781
that you both do in terms of advocacy

00:03:10.781 --> 00:03:13.090
and just in your day to day endeavors.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:14.860
So, as my listeners know,

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.693
I do like to check in

00:03:15.693 --> 00:03:16.930
and see how people are doing,

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.080
especially with,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.120
some of the heavy topics

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.550
that you all are engaged in

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.510
on the daily basis as well.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:23.343
So we want to start there

00:03:23.343 --> 00:03:25.020
and just see how you're doing

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.940
today of all days and just,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.640
in our ongoing changing
societal situation as it were.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.980
So Matt, why don't we go
ahead and start with you?

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.720
- Yeah, I appreciate the question too,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.300
'cause I think we don't know

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.510
what's going on for folks

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.510
in the midst of these 20 months.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:42.860
And I think I learned
early on in the pandemic

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.870
that I didn't know how I
was gonna feel each day

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.070
until my feet hit the floor

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.040
in the morning when I got up.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.760
So today I'm feeling really excited,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.223
energized, and hopeful.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.190
And also at the same time,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:56.920
balance that with thinking about

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.950
what these next few months and winter

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.640
is gonna bring both as it relates

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.710
to social isolation

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.180
and really being a part again

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.710
and pandemic surges.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.070
But I'm balancing that
keeping that in my mind

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:11.700
while also at the same time,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.433
feeling really grateful and excited.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.273
- That's great.

00:04:16.273 --> 00:04:17.730
And in a sense,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:18.900
also just appreciate the way

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:19.750
that you talked about that

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.860
even in your rotation and in your role,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.140
I think it's helpful for people to realize

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.870
that even those who are
serving in organizations,

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.320
who are in the mental health space

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.520
doesn't mean that you have

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:29.950
all the answers all the times.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:30.820
I mean, as you talked about

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.060
just not knowing how you'd feel

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.780
on a specific day and to
give yourself permission

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.910
to have that unknown in a sense,

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.630
I think is really important.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.463
- Yeah.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.330
- Corey, what about you?

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.430
How are you at this moment?

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.950
- Well, I say ditto,

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.360
Matt covered it all.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:48.880
It's very important for us

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.000
to take the time to think about ourselves

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.140
when we help people,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.410
other people on a daily basis sometimes,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.110
we forget or perhaps

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:59.610
just don't make ourselves a priority

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.940
and it's important to do that.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.840
And for me right now, just grateful,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.380
not just because it's November

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.740
and we're going into thanksgiving,

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.760
but just overall grateful

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.360
for safety and good health,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.630
battling a little cold,

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.370
but that's nothing in comparison

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.930
to other things that
are going on right now.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.030
And so, I'm just grateful

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.700
and appreciate the opportunity

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.700
to continue to share
with social isolation,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.100
by having Zooms and things
that can keep us safe,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.920
but just understanding that it's important

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.210
to still do the work,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:33.680
but we have to make sure

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.430
that we are keeping ourselves energized

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.830
and healthy in order to
continue to do the work.

00:05:40.881 --> 00:05:42.283
- That's so well said and so important.

00:05:42.283 --> 00:05:44.730
I appreciate the way
that you all are bringing

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.190
just these, I mean, the perspectives,

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:49.310
the gratefulness kind of
the reality of the unknown.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.000
I think that that's just healthy

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:51.970
for us to be able to have,

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.100
even these types of conversation.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:55.680
As leaders to say, okay,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.710
there are things we can look back

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:57.730
and be grateful for,

00:05:57.730 --> 00:05:59.170
but there are also things that we

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:00.880
know are still challenging even as we have

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.930
that attitude of
gratefulness moving forward.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.360
So definitely appreciate

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.650
the standard and the example

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.130
that you all are living too.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.880
And I know that doesn't mean it's easy.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:09.983
- Oh absolutely not.

00:06:12.628 --> 00:06:15.534
But you know that going in.

00:06:15.534 --> 00:06:16.367
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:18.350
And I think, the pandemic really

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.580
kind of shined a light

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:23.250
on how easy it is to forget,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.070
how difficult it can be.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:26.903
I consider myself

00:06:26.903 --> 00:06:29.250
to be a pretty resilient individual

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.270
and the struggle has been real

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.490
and really up and down,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.840
despite feeling incredibly grateful

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:35.960
and fortunate, I've had my health,

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:37.640
I have not had COVID-19,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.970
I'm lucky enough to be in a safe house

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.540
and have access to everything that I need.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.620
And this pandemic really forced me

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:46.860
to kind of dig deeper and say,

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.360
well, if I'm struggling

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.270
and I see myself as pretty resilient,

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.440
what must this be like for everyone?

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:54.720
And I think a lot of times,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.710
we hear people talk about

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:00.320
that we're all in the same
boat going through this.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.530
And I think we're all
experiencing coronavirus

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:04.700
at the same time,

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.763
but our boats look really different.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.130
Some of us are on a super yacht,

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.180
some of us are on a raft
that's falling apart,

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:16.540
some of us are by ourselves, in a kayak,

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.810
some of us are with
friends on a speed boat.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.780
I mean, we're all on the riding

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.583
the Corona river as it were.

00:07:24.583 --> 00:07:26.727
But the journey is very different,

00:07:26.727 --> 00:07:29.850
and I'm so grateful for that perspective

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.500
because I didn't see it
really in that same way

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.083
prior to all this happening.

00:07:35.573 --> 00:07:36.406
- I think that's saying a lot.

00:07:36.406 --> 00:07:37.410
I appreciate that honesty as well,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.780
because even in your position,

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.190
as someone who thinks about these topics,

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.590
often even things that
you're also learning

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.410
and seeing as we're going
through the pandemic as well.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:48.790
Corey, I wonder if in your work,

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:50.240
as you've been involved
in advocacy as well,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.440
have you seen any shifts

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.120
as we've kind of hit the pandemic

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.110
as we try to think about mental health?

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.710
Obviously there's been more
attention as Matt mentioned

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:58.810
to the mental health piece,

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.460
but any shifts that you've noticed?

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.483
- Yes, surely an increase indeed.

00:08:02.483 --> 00:08:04.160
And that's why I'm very grateful

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.610
for organizations like NAMI,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.140
the National Alliance on Mental Illness

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.390
that have transitioned with

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:10.810
our world transitioning

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.550
and having these opportunities

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:16.510
for virtual classes, virtual seminars,

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:17.920
virtual trainings,

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.100
because mental illness

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.640
or mental health conditions do not stop

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.360
because everything else
in our world has stopped

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.570
and people need the services

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:29.930
and their medication

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.720
and their support systems, the act team,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.010
anyone has the acting
assertive community treatment

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.260
teams that come out and help.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.780
This pandemic doesn't stop those needs.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:43.160
And I'm really grateful for organizations

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:45.000
that found a way to continue

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.030
to keep their employees safe

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:48.690
and provide the services

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.414
to individuals that need it.

00:08:50.414 --> 00:08:53.620
And that's for those who already

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.430
were established with the services.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.450
What we see now are people
trying to deal with,

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:00.230
grappling with their feelings

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.040
and emotions and losing their job

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.450
or losing family members.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.760
And they don't know how to
deal with their emotions

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:06.730
and how they're feeling

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.480
and may not know about
any of these organizations

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.060
and using them for the first time,

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.410
like Food Banks.

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:15.270
But being grateful that
these things are here

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:17.480
and maybe they don't
want to have to use them,

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:18.790
but they know it's there

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.260
and they can use them without shame.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.353
So I think it's very important

00:09:22.353 --> 00:09:24.760
that as we see this increase,

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.890
we know there was already a need.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:28.860
And then that helps us as advocates

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:30.490
to continue to advocate

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.640
for more services,

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.570
more funding, more treatment,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:34.700
because it doesn't stop,

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.660
if anything, it's going to increase.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.360
- Yeah, yeah, I think
you're exactly right.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.030
And I love how you pointed,

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.890
paying that as kind of two different

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:42.850
fore signs in a sense,

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.630
really pointing people to
the resources are there,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.300
but also making sure more resources

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.840
are available in that way as well.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.050
And that's a helpful segue as well.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.010
'Cause Max I was gonna have you

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:54.100
just actually talk a little bit about,

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:55.140
how you got into this work

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.630
and what actual services NAMI provides

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.920
for any who may not be familiar

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:00.920
only vaguely familiar

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.470
with some of the work that you all do.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:05.480
- Yeah, so I've been
with NAMI New York city

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.320
for a little over four years.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.750
And prior to that,

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.560
I worked with older adults

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.330
and their caregivers

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.530
with a focus on Alzheimer's disease

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.550
and other neurocognitive disorders.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.700
So I worked in aging for about 15 years

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:20.450
and then the opportunity came

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:23.410
to move to NAMI NYC and people would say,

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:24.263
oh, why, you know, you've been doing,

00:10:24.263 --> 00:10:25.820
working in aging for so long,

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:27.610
why are you doing this 180

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.790
and going into this whole other space?

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:30.623
And it really isn't a 180,

00:10:30.623 --> 00:10:34.030
there's so much more in
common with Alzheimer's,

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:38.500
especially it's not a mental illness,

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:41.030
but the stigma is profound.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:43.120
The shame that it brings to families

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.510
is just as powerful.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.457
The ability for the person

00:10:47.457 --> 00:10:50.140
who is living with the illness

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.640
to participate and play an active role

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:53.480
in their wellness,

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.191
their recovery, their wellbeing,

00:10:55.191 --> 00:10:59.711
is meaningfully impacted by each of these

00:10:59.711 --> 00:11:01.180
and the impact on families

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.950
and caregivers and
communities more generally

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.400
is really similar.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.230
And so what I found at NAMI NYC

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.030
that's so special

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.380
is that we are a peer organization.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:12.840
So the mental health space

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.220
is a really crowded space

00:11:15.343 --> 00:11:17.100
and organizations like ours

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.693
that are focused on individuals

00:11:18.693 --> 00:11:19.526
with lived experience,

00:11:19.526 --> 00:11:21.960
both people living with
mental health issues

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:23.470
and family members, friends,

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.290
and other supporters,

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:26.330
coming together to share

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:27.820
their personal experiences,

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.230
their journeys,

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:31.030
the validation that they can share

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:32.740
from understanding what
you're going through

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.270
because they are living it as well.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:38.500
It's outside the treatment modality.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.240
So, but it also compliments

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.590
what we do this peer support model

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.910
is complimentary to every one of those

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.760
more traditional treatment
kinds of intervention.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.010
So, you can go to therapy once a week,

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.620
you can be served by an act team.

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:56.300
You can in a clubhouse program

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.410
and still come to NAMI NYC,

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.620
get support, get
connected, find community,

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.410
and a sense of belonging,

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.300
which can be really difficult.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.010
I think so often when
people are struggling

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:09.300
with mental health issues,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.590
they and their family members,

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.860
feel so isolated and alone.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.770
And we're here, we've lived it,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.017
we are living it.

00:12:16.017 --> 00:12:17.660
And when you walk through our door

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:21.360
or you pop into one of
our virtual sessions,

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.650
you're welcomed and you see

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:25.790
that the playing field is level,

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.050
that there's really a safety

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.530
and a nonjudgmental approach

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:30.860
to the work we do.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:33.180
For us things really
start with our helpline,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.780
it's available Monday through Friday

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:36.540
from 10:00 AM to 6:00 PM.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.220
It's the primary way that
folks first connect with us.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:40.711
I like to say, you don't need to know

00:12:40.711 --> 00:12:42.300
what questions to ask,

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.860
just call and our helpline responders

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.447
can help you figure out what's going on

00:12:46.447 --> 00:12:49.180
and the best way to kind
of navigate your situation.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.130
I think we have to remember,

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.060
and if you've met one
person who's living with

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:54.670
or affected by mental illness,

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:55.870
you've met one person.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:59.820
These are not easy situations to solve

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.000
with a one size fits all approach,

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:05.181
it's very individualized
and person centered.

00:13:05.181 --> 00:13:07.230
And so we'd like to start where people are

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:09.130
and connect them both internally

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:11.530
to our full array of
programs and services,

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.990
all of which are available free of charge

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:14.940
and also connect them

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.910
to very specific, meaningful resources

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.680
that may exist outside of our walls.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.760
So we keep a really
robust resource directory

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.190
of over 500 entries of programs

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.890
and services and resources

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.450
within the New York city area

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:30.250
for different community members,

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:31.910
for diverse communities,

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:33.250
for different kinds of needs,

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.850
whether it's legal assistance or housing

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.400
or any other employment,

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:38.233
anything that you can think of

00:13:38.233 --> 00:13:39.770
really intersecting with us.

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:41.630
We wanna be a trusted resource

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.779
of people who understand and get it

00:13:43.779 --> 00:13:46.957
because of that lived experience.

00:13:46.957 --> 00:13:48.560
And from the helpline
that really kicks off

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.240
to evidence-based education classes.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.730
We have over 30 support groups

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.410
that take place every single month.

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.340
We do a lot of stigma reduction work,

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:57.510
we go into schools

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.880
and community centers and companies.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.940
And we do a ton of advocacy

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:04.700
and we're recruiting folks

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:06.580
to lend their voices to these issues,

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:08.490
and these causes, to help shine a light

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:10.180
on how many of us are affected.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.470
We say, one out of five of us

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:12.980
is living with mental illness

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:14.910
while the other four are
their family members,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.840
their friends, their colleagues at work,

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:18.380
their social networks,

00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:19.990
their next door neighbors.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:22.060
So these issues really affect all of us.

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:23.990
And when we find a way to talk about

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:24.823
what we're going through,

00:14:24.823 --> 00:14:26.290
we realize we're not alone.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:28.580
We realize we are part of a community

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:30.590
that is deeply impacted in these ways

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:32.690
that other people can really understand,

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.300
appreciate and validate.

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.133
- That's so good, I mean,

00:14:36.133 --> 00:14:38.100
it's such an array of just opportunities.

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:39.030
And I love the framing too.

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:40.410
That sounds like a lot of ways,

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:41.470
it's an entry point.

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:42.303
The fact that you're saying

00:14:42.303 --> 00:14:44.020
people don't need to know what to ask,

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.370
I think is powerful.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.670
'Cause I think sometimes
there can be a hesitation,

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:49.800
not knowing what to ask, even if it's,

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:51.050
someone that you know, who's struggling

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.850
or someone, you may be
struggling yourself,

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:53.750
not knowing where to start,

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:56.540
but to be able to have
that level of openness,

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:57.530
to try and figure out

00:14:57.530 --> 00:14:58.363
what to ask the first place,

00:14:58.363 --> 00:14:59.710
then to be able to be plugged

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:02.380
into all the different
resources and programs

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:03.213
that you all have

00:15:03.213 --> 00:15:04.200
and you connect other people with too,

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.560
I think that's really powerful

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:07.360
and we definitely need more of it.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:09.930
- Yeah, and I think
being treatment adjacent

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:12.933
and having as wider front door as we can,

00:15:12.933 --> 00:15:15.930
with as few barriers as possible

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.840
and really being of the communities

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.720
that we're connecting and serving,

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.407
allows us to create a space

00:15:21.407 --> 00:15:23.370
where you don't have to know the answers,

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.090
you don't have to.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:26.140
We just want you to call,

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:27.190
we just want you to come,

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:29.820
just connect with us to recognize

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.520
that this is a journey.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.643
These are marathons, not sprints.

00:15:34.585 --> 00:15:36.140
And anything we can do

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.900
to provide that kind of support.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:39.750
I think sometimes we
live in a world today,

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.740
where it's a little
dangerous to make mistakes

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:44.420
in some ways to say the wrong thing

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.634
or to use the wrong word.

00:15:46.634 --> 00:15:49.164
And we wanna create a space where people

00:15:49.164 --> 00:15:51.695
can feel vulnerable and safe,

00:15:51.695 --> 00:15:53.665
exploring these issues

00:15:53.665 --> 00:15:54.615
to figure it out

00:15:54.615 --> 00:15:55.960
because they're so complex

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:57.350
and there's so much nuance

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.570
and it's so individualized

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:02.330
that I don't want
anybody to feel the shame

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:05.140
that they can't sort of
discover what's going on,

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:07.870
discover on this journey
of how to help someone,

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:09.130
how to help yourself

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:10.580
while you're helping someone.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:12.820
So we really try to just be

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:14.570
as open as we possibly can.

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:15.600
- Yeah, and I really love that.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:17.040
I mean, the way that
you're inviting people in,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:18.160
'cause it's tough already.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:20.770
So, that piece is huge

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:23.300
to be able to have that
openness and invitation

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.160
to really include so many folks

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:26.123
in so many ways.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:28.710
Corey, to pivot to you,

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.370
I know you've also been involved in NAMI,

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:32.270
but then also have a bit of your own story

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.790
that you've been walking
through even before,

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.380
you got involved in the organization.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:37.390
So I wonder if you could just tell us

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:39.930
a little bit about your
path into this work

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.530
and the ongoing work that
you're doing as well.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.210
- Yeah, I heard you say walking through

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.210
and I feel like I was
thrown into this space,

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:50.180
and I had to figure it out,

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.890
like a fish out of water flapping around

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.940
because I was around 14 years old

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:58.030
when we had to take my mother

00:16:58.030 --> 00:16:59.770
to the emergency room,

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:03.373
she was talking non-stop, incoherent.

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:07.013
She had the ideas of grandiose,

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.390
thinking that she had miraculous powers

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:11.770
and all of these things.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:14.460
And so for me to hear that,

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.410
I did not know what was going on

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:20.180
and for me to not be
able to interact with her

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.870
because it was, I'm talking to her,

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.310
but she wasn't talking to me

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.610
and it was like, I wasn't there.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:27.190
But a family member came

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:29.010
and we went to the emergency room

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.560
and that's when I heard the diagnosis

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:31.840
for the first time,

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.390
manic depression, paranoid schizophrenia.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:36.490
And manic depression now known as,

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.640
bipolar disorder.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.030
But still those were just words,

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:42.630
didn't know what that meant,

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:44.723
didn't know that that
would ultimately mean

00:17:44.723 --> 00:17:47.000
that she wouldn't be the mother

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.050
that I thought that I had

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:52.560
or would have for the rest of my life,

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.007
but it was in the learning experience.

00:17:55.007 --> 00:17:57.600
And so from that age of 14,

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.340
basically seeing my mother go in and out

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:02.200
of mental health institutions,

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.550
and by the time I went to college,

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:07.020
I just was trying to
learn all that I could.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:11.340
And that my first education
piece, if you would,

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.350
is a television movie
called "Out Of Darkness",

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:15.370
that starred Diana Ross,

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.190
it came out in 1994.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.600
But when I saw the previews,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.500
I'm like, it looked very, very familiar

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:21.333
and I'm like, oh my gosh,

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:22.840
I need to watch this.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:24.700
But I didn't know that
that was mental illness,

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:25.800
I didn't know that that was

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.000
some kind of way related
to my mother's diagnosis,

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.570
but as I continue to educate myself

00:18:32.570 --> 00:18:34.320
and then getting a master's degree,

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.850
that wasn't because I
wanted it necessarily.

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:39.190
I wanted to learn more
about mental illness

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.130
and what my mom was going through.

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:44.010
And studying from the DSM-IV at that time,

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:45.280
the five is out now.

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:46.850
But that helped tremendously

00:18:46.850 --> 00:18:48.257
and learning a bit more

00:18:48.257 --> 00:18:50.270
and what could happen,

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:52.820
what was available for my mom's future,

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:53.790
as well as mine.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.590
So with that,

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:57.580
once I started my career as a lawyer,

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:58.900
my aunt, my mother's sister,

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:00.100
who was very instrumental

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:01.870
in helping my mother as well.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:05.530
She saw an article for
NAMI in the newspaper.

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:06.580
She clipped it out

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:07.810
and put it in my mailbox.

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.630
And she had a little note on it saying,

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.303
I think this is something
you should go to.

00:19:13.192 --> 00:19:15.077
And it was the family, the family,

00:19:15.077 --> 00:19:16.790
the course it was 12 weeks

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:19.010
at that time back in 2009.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:20.910
And I went to it,

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.120
I think I was probably
the youngest person there,

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:24.150
the only person there

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.120
who was there because of a parent

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.760
with severe mental illness,

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:32.380
but all of our stories were
so similar, so similar.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.100
And we learned a lot from each other

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.570
as well as the course.

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:38.120
And then as I continued in my career

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:40.900
and most of my last 10 years of my career

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:42.440
was in public housing.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.620
And we provide housing for individuals

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:47.600
with disabilities and low
income families and seniors.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.730
So dealing with reasonable accommodations

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:53.330
and other things that
are on the legal side

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.320
with giving me a whole nother aspect

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.480
of advocacy for individuals

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.670
who are living with severe mental illness.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.420
Ultimately, I started
helping to raise money

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.960
for our local NAMI chapter

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.690
through the walks that we would have.

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:10.510
And I then wanted to get
trained and give back.

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:11.500
It was my way,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:14.280
someone took time out to provide

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.030
these free services for me and my family

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:18.380
and other families,

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:21.150
and I wanted to be able to do the same.

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:22.670
So I did do the training.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:25.230
I co-facilitated my first course,

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:27.950
and I also participated for the first time

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:29.390
and speaking to law enforcement

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:32.070
as a part of their in-service training

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:34.210
and sharing our lived experience

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:37.320
and helping them to have strict strategies

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:38.410
in place procedures,

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.670
in place for helping someone in crisis

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:42.270
and things that they can do

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.640
and working with the family.

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:45.710
So I think that's so important,

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:47.270
especially in the black community

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:49.500
and we have concerns
of calling the police.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:50.333
We want help,

00:20:50.333 --> 00:20:52.020
but you don't know if anybody

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.950
is gonna make it alive.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:57.300
And those are realities
for us, unfortunately.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.020
And I know I've had to call the police

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:00.630
a number of times on my mother

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:05.370
and today looking back on it, I feel fear,

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:06.690
but I'm grateful to God

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.480
that nothing ever happened to my mother

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:12.200
and that we had law enforcement officers

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:15.670
and, individuals from
community treatment teams

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:19.200
that helped even when
they didn't have to help.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.980
I kind of feel like I had an
informal network of people

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.220
because they knew me,

00:21:25.220 --> 00:21:26.950
they had my number, they could call me

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:30.040
and I could call them to try ultimately

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:31.210
to keep my mother safe.

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:32.740
Even when her behaviors

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:34.793
weren't so safe towards me.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:37.093
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:21:37.093 --> 00:21:40.330
I mean, there's so many just key pieces

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:41.420
that you just talked about.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:43.360
I mean, from the legal aspect,

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.090
from what you've gone through

00:21:46.198 --> 00:21:47.870
as a family member,

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:48.940
to just having that support

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:50.800
and having teams in place.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:53.610
I think it's so encouraging to hear

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.470
the way that you have

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:56.440
walked it out in a sense,

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:57.800
and I know walking isn't,

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:58.740
not the best way to put it

00:21:58.740 --> 00:21:59.573
because as you mentioned,

00:21:59.573 --> 00:22:00.406
you were thrown into it.

00:22:00.406 --> 00:22:02.380
But the fact that you've been able to

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:04.310
take that throwing and
turn it into a journey

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:05.890
that's really empowering so many people

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:10.750
I think is really
amazing in a lot of ways.

00:22:10.750 --> 00:22:13.030
I know, having heard you
talk elsewhere before

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:14.300
that you definitely are a proponent

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:15.133
of the team approach

00:22:15.133 --> 00:22:17.007
and how important that has been as well.

00:22:17.007 --> 00:22:18.070
And I think that's a theme

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:18.980
that cuts through the work

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:21.100
that both of you do in terms of the,

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:22.700
these aren't things that
are done in isolation.

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:23.940
And a lot of ways, you're trying

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:26.660
to get folks out of that isolation

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:27.930
to make sure that there are ways

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:31.350
to really approach this as teams.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:32.183
But I'm also curious

00:22:32.183 --> 00:22:33.760
because both of you are so involved

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.780
in the work itself.

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:37.360
Do you think that permeates?

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:38.610
So, I mean, as we were talking about

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:39.770
before we came on,

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:40.670
and Matt, you mentioned as well,

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:43.730
one in five who are
dealing with mental illness

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:46.220
and the other four probably
know someone who is.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:47.700
But then I feel like there's also

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:48.740
a difference between those

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:51.170
who are actively in the midst of it

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.420
and have all of these components

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:54.253
that you've talked about

00:22:54.253 --> 00:22:55.750
and think about these things deeply

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.670
versus some who are in the
midst of it at the moment

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.300
and may have perspectives on the outside.

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:02.340
So when we talk about

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:04.550
how mental health is viewed as society,

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:05.840
do you feel like there's a difference

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.460
between those who are kind of in the midst

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:10.920
versus the general portrayal in society?

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:12.990
And if so, are we making progress

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:14.050
in actually improving that

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:16.673
and making those two a
little bit more aligned?

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:19.500
So I know that's a big question, but...

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:21.760
- From what I hear

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:22.920
and from what you're saying,

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.670
I really feel that those, any of us,

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:27.210
those who were in the work or not

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:30.060
we're part of the one in five we're apart,

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:32.080
'cause that's any mental illness.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:35.510
Depression and that anything that causes,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:38.470
and I'm not a clinician, I'm a lawyer,

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.660
but anything that causes heightened,

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:44.493
emotional, something,

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:47.963
like it could be good
stress or bad stress.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:50.210
Planning a wedding,

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.240
that can be great,

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:53.750
but it's also stressful.

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:55.880
But it's a good stress, right?

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:59.080
Having a death or having issues at work

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:01.290
that could be like bad stress.

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:03.800
But depending on how it impacts you,

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:07.020
it can be from mild to severe.

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:08.440
It just would like depression.

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:10.110
So, you're not eating well,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:11.460
you're not sleeping well,

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:13.030
whatever your symptoms may be.

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:16.320
And I feel like that is the one in five.

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:17.990
But severe mental illness,

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:21.490
one in 25 experience
severe mental illness.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:24.180
And that's the biggest distinctions

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:28.030
that I recognize

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:31.280
and are the hallucinations,

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:33.810
delusions and anosognosia,

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:37.030
where the person doesn't even have

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:39.440
knowledge that they're sick.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:42.280
And that can be very, very dangerous.

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.060
It's very, very difficult

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:46.550
because you try to communicate

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:47.800
with your loved one,

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.240
you see the behaviors,

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:51.110
they don't see the behaviors,

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:53.620
and it can just be very, very complicated.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:55.610
But the biggest distinction for me,

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:57.710
for any mental illness

00:24:57.710 --> 00:24:59.510
versus severe mental illness,

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.000
when you're dealing
with the hallucinations

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.110
and the delusions,

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:04.930
and anosognosia.

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:07.920
Any other thing that's impacting mentally

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.660
are things that we can perhaps have

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:12.780
outside treatment for.

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:15.080
We may not necessarily need medication,

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:18.390
or we may need to invoke more self care

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.250
and you be able to deal with it.

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:22.840
But self care alone is not gonna deal

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:24.700
with severe mental illness.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.700
And so I just caution people as caregivers

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:30.320
because the statistics are alarming

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:34.590
for caregivers and their
own health declining

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:36.680
because of the stress and strain

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:39.260
of helping a whole nother person

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:40.740
and that person's life,

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:43.120
in addition to your own life,

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:44.980
work, children, family,

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:46.930
whatever your responsibilities are

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:48.950
and that of another person

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.920
and their hallucinations or delusions,

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:52.830
if you're dealing with
severe mental illness.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.220
But even with depression,

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.350
if it may be in its most severe forms,

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:59.270
causes a lot of stress and strain

00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:00.960
on the caregiver as well.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:01.793
So with that,

00:26:01.793 --> 00:26:03.930
I encourage people in whatever work

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.070
that they're doing,

00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:06.280
that they implement,

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:08.790
what is good for them
in terms of self care.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:10.660
That's not just going to the spa

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:11.960
and things of that nature,

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:13.160
but meditation.

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:14.930
And sometimes it's just chilling,

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:17.010
like just chill out,

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.685
take a seat somewhere and chill out.

00:26:19.685 --> 00:26:20.518
- That is really well said.

00:26:20.518 --> 00:26:21.950
Do you have other specific tools

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.060
that you've encouraged folks to use,

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:25.990
or even things that you've used yourself

00:26:25.990 --> 00:26:27.330
over the years?

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:30.550
- Yes, like walking, sitting by water,

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:31.383
you can listen.

00:26:31.383 --> 00:26:33.150
There's so many apps
that are available now

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:36.600
to like calm, Headspace,

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:37.640
different things like that.

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:40.130
But what I found and even counseling

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.510
and is because of my busy-ness,

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:46.513
I needed to combat down and be consistent.

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:50.970
For example, it took me three times

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:52.000
to go to counseling

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:53.870
and for me to commit to it.

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:55.360
Once when I was a child,

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:57.427
unfortunately my mother did attack me

00:26:57.427 --> 00:26:58.800
and I was removed from the home

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:00.030
and so part of the court order

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:02.620
was for me to go to counseling,

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:03.453
I didn't like it.

00:27:03.453 --> 00:27:05.370
I might've went to two or three sessions.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:07.760
I share this because it's okay

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:10.430
if your first time doesn't work out,

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:12.140
you know, just try it again.

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.410
Maybe that counselor

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.590
and you don't jell, that's fine.

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:18.220
You don't have to go to just one.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:20.040
Another time was when I was in law school

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:23.870
and I may have had like
mild depression then

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:25.600
based on the survey.

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:28.860
But all of my responsibility at that time,

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:30.450
you would think that,

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:31.827
you probably are stressed out,

00:27:31.827 --> 00:27:34.060
it causes some level of depression.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:35.130
I was a new mother,

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:36.290
my son was potty training

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:37.650
when I started law school.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:39.367
New wife, commuting to law school

00:27:39.367 --> 00:27:42.430
and law school in general was just hard.

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:45.070
So I did try it, but maybe one session,

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:46.330
I did not like it.

00:27:46.330 --> 00:27:47.910
But the third time,

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:48.890
I'm still with her

00:27:48.890 --> 00:27:51.580
today, has been about at least 10 years.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.720
But what made it effective,

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:55.590
was when I made the commitment

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:58.060
to be consistent with my appointments

00:27:58.060 --> 00:28:00.040
because of everything else I was doing,

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:02.300
counseling would be the
first thing I would cancel.

00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:05.580
And I had to learn to
not operate at a crisis,

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.490
not to try to get an appointment

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:09.280
when I'm overwhelmed with

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:11.610
whatever I was dealing with in my life.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.590
But just having it as a
constant on my schedule,

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:17.128
not canceling it and going.

00:28:17.128 --> 00:28:18.240
- Yeah, and that's so important.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:19.490
All those things that you brought out,

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:20.430
just in terms of,

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:21.750
it doesn't always work on the first time,

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:22.710
like you said,

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:24.210
having that persistence and willingness

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.690
to kind of make it a priority too,

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:28.160
which is also not easy and you're right,

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.433
I mean, people don't always jell.

00:28:30.433 --> 00:28:31.630
So I think to your point,

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:32.660
people shouldn't be disappointed

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:34.423
if it doesn't work out the first time.

00:28:36.553 --> 00:28:37.947
And people have to be in the right,

00:28:37.947 --> 00:28:40.138
has to be the right combination.

00:28:40.138 --> 00:28:41.420
This is in terms of personality

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:43.200
and all the different types of styles

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:44.100
of different therapists use.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:45.430
So just being open to saying,

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:46.263
okay, that style,

00:28:46.263 --> 00:28:47.810
that person might not
have been the right fit,

00:28:47.810 --> 00:28:50.270
that doesn't mean we
throw it all out per se.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:51.967
So your example, I think is great.

00:28:51.967 --> 00:28:54.583
- I think it's like dating a little bit.

00:28:56.290 --> 00:28:57.173
It's about that fit,

00:28:57.173 --> 00:28:58.510
it's about that fit

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:00.080
and not everybody is gonna be

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:02.150
what you need in that moment.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:03.870
And I also think that,

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:05.140
when you think about therapy,

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:08.170
it tends to be a white middle upper class

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:10.200
sort of construct

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:11.850
that other people from other cultures

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.710
and other diverse groups,

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:15.410
aren't used to going to a stranger

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:16.630
and talking about

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:17.840
what's going on inside of them,

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:19.650
that they've maybe never
said out loud before

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:20.870
to someone else.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:23.030
So not only do we have
to help normalize that,

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:24.900
it's okay to talk with people

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:26.950
who are expert and trained

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:29.361
to help guide you through it,

00:29:29.361 --> 00:29:31.591
which is hard to convince people.

00:29:31.591 --> 00:29:32.740
Oh, we don't talk about

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:34.010
that stuff outside the family

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:37.102
or we got this, this is
our problem to solve,

00:29:37.102 --> 00:29:38.452
thanks anyway.

00:29:38.452 --> 00:29:40.180
But then you have to get people to realize

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:42.804
that it might not on that first try

00:29:42.804 --> 00:29:45.562
and know how important
it is to find someone

00:29:45.562 --> 00:29:47.364
who understands your experience.

00:29:47.364 --> 00:29:48.197
So that when you're talking about

00:29:48.197 --> 00:29:50.701
what's going on for you, they may not,

00:29:50.701 --> 00:29:52.242
they don't have to have literally

00:29:52.242 --> 00:29:53.867
experienced it themselves.

00:29:53.867 --> 00:29:56.380
But if they don't understand the culture,

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:59.330
the context of where you're coming from,

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:02.090
it's gonna be a lot
harder to really feel like

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:04.480
you're obscene and heard in a way.

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:06.480
And you don't wanna have to explain,

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:07.660
like, I'm a gay man.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:08.630
I don't wanna have to explain

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:09.557
what that means

00:30:09.557 --> 00:30:11.773
to my straight therapist.

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:15.146
It's a different starting point,

00:30:15.146 --> 00:30:17.834
and I think it's so important.

00:30:17.834 --> 00:30:18.797
I know a lot of people said,

00:30:18.797 --> 00:30:20.010
oh, I tried it once,

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:22.520
not for me therapy, not for me.

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:23.630
You gotta try it again,

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:25.260
I think it's really important.

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:26.390
Like Corey said,

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:27.890
it may not be that first shot

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:28.924
for whatever reason.

00:30:28.924 --> 00:30:29.757
Maybe it's not the person,

00:30:29.757 --> 00:30:31.580
maybe you're not ready for it,

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:35.500
but you gotta keep giving
it a shot if you can

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:36.670
I think, yeah.

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:37.503
- So well said.

00:30:37.503 --> 00:30:38.430
And I really appreciate that,

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.680
in your role too, that
you're thinking about,

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:42.480
that cultural competency
life you talked about

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.520
so that people can
engage with you on that,

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:47.230
to know that if that's missing,

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:48.870
that's a huge component

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.650
that can really be, that's important

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:52.540
and needs to be part of it.

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:53.950
So the fact that you,

00:30:53.950 --> 00:30:55.927
you are making that plain

00:30:55.927 --> 00:30:56.760
and making it known,

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.660
I think it's really important as well.

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:00.640
Oh, go ahead.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:03.510
- I was just gonna say
these differences matter.

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:04.620
They really make it,

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:06.660
it matters a lot.

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:08.890
After George Floyd was murdered,

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:11.810
we started a group
called Black Minds Matter

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:13.320
to create a space for people

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:14.560
who identify as black

00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:17.050
and living with a mental health condition,

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:18.500
to come together

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:21.203
among those multiple levels
of shared experience.

00:31:22.959 --> 00:31:25.960
And we heard from a
lot of people early on,

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:27.410
I've been waiting

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:29.850
