00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.380
which I think is really important.

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:21.620
- And what I also found important

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:22.850
just in the community,

00:33:22.850 --> 00:33:25.350
'cause some people don't
wanna identify with

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:28.790
how they know you in the community.

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:29.810
But once I guess,

00:33:29.810 --> 00:33:31.520
whether it's like an eye contact

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:34.360
or it's okay, I wanna give you the,

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:35.900
'cause we know each other from NAMI.

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:37.610
You wanna make sure that you are

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:40.620
respecting people's privacy

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:42.490
and what they are willing to share

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:45.067
outside of the space,

00:33:45.067 --> 00:33:46.630
that's important as well.

00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:49.090
But it's great to still have connections

00:33:49.090 --> 00:33:50.800
with people who went through the program.

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:51.960
- Yeah, that's great to hear

00:33:51.960 --> 00:33:53.860
those continue as well.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:55.242
And Matt that's encouraging.

00:33:55.242 --> 00:33:56.075
Yeah, go ahead.

00:33:56.075 --> 00:33:57.710
- I was just gonna say something

00:33:57.710 --> 00:34:00.490
that goes back to the,
your question, Dr.Addy

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:03.423
around kind of the state of things now.

00:34:04.515 --> 00:34:06.420
And Corey, as you were talking

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:09.810
about the distinction
between mental illness

00:34:09.810 --> 00:34:11.763
and serious mental illness.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:14.920
One of the things that

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:17.200
we've been talking a lot about,

00:34:17.200 --> 00:34:19.730
these issues they're not binary,

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:21.560
it's not all or nothing.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:23.773
It's far more spectrum than it is,

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:31.830
a binary, an all or
nothing, kind of a thinking.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:36.560
So you can have optimal mental health

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:39.050
and still have experienced symptoms

00:34:39.050 --> 00:34:40.340
of mental health challenges.

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:43.210
You can be living with them diagnosis

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:44.270
of serious mental illness

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.240
and have really great

00:34:46.240 --> 00:34:48.830
positive, strong mental health.

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:52.550
So, the plot points on
that build quadrants,

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:55.310
really, I think how people see

00:34:55.310 --> 00:34:56.530
that if you have a brain,

00:34:56.530 --> 00:34:57.727
you have mental health

00:34:57.727 --> 00:35:00.110
and your mental health
is gonna be impacted

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:02.680
in whatever goes on throughout your day.

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:04.903
In positive ways as Corey was saying,

00:35:04.903 --> 00:35:07.310
and in not so positive ways.

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:09.660
And I think everybody being forced

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:11.410
to kind of live through this,

00:35:11.410 --> 00:35:14.060
I hope, and it feels as if

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:15.570
there's a little bit of a groundswell

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.748
toward recognition that
it isn't us and them,

00:35:19.748 --> 00:35:21.420
that we're all on that spectrum,

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:24.040
we're all within that continuum

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:25.893
of that framework.

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:29.350
And how it doesn't take much

00:35:29.350 --> 00:35:31.790
to negatively impact your mental health

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:33.820
wherever you are at baseline,

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:34.653
whether it's living

00:35:34.653 --> 00:35:36.593
with serious mental illness or not.

00:35:38.180 --> 00:35:42.970
And I hope that that
has positively impacted

00:35:44.870 --> 00:35:46.550
the space through,

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:51.440
by people understanding

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:56.440
how universal it really is.

00:35:57.437 --> 00:35:58.390
- Right, it is.

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:00.750
I mean, 'cause it doesn't discriminate.

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:04.400
And I love that you use
the brain just saying it

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:05.943
'cause I just said this
in a speech somewhere.

00:36:05.943 --> 00:36:08.780
It's like anybody that has a brain

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:10.590
has mental health.

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:12.220
And then there's the condition of it.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:13.800
So I think about legal forms

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:14.633
that we have to fill out,

00:36:14.633 --> 00:36:16.030
especially with divorces.

00:36:16.030 --> 00:36:18.700
And one of the questions
is about your health.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:19.840
What would you put?

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:21.653
Poor, good, excellent.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:24.330
That's essentially what the surveys are.

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:26.290
And depending on what your numbers are

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:27.950
that could tell you whether you're mild

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:29.210
to severe and depression

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:30.860
or whatever it could be.

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:32.010
And I would love it

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:35.560
if we could just have this more openness

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:38.700
and it really does attribute to stigma.

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:39.790
Stigma, it seems like

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:42.750
as soon as you say the word mental,

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:45.313
we all pause, what are you gonna say next?

00:36:45.313 --> 00:36:46.910
'Cause I'm not connecting with that word.

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:48.860
What are you gonna say?

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:51.180
But we have to understand,

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.430
people are so open to blurting out

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.300
what their physical conditions are,

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:56.960
the physical ailments and whatnot,

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:58.750
but not so much with the mental.

00:36:58.750 --> 00:36:59.830
And so I say, okay,

00:36:59.830 --> 00:37:02.420
just take mental and physical out of it.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:04.890
I'm having some health complications.

00:37:04.890 --> 00:37:06.760
Do we need to know us with your mind

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:08.300
or with some other part of your body?

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:10.600
No, that's between you and your doctor.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.840
But what I am encouraging people,

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:14.740
the words of Bebe Moore Campbell,

00:37:14.740 --> 00:37:15.840
she was a three-time,

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:17.360
New York Times bestselling author.

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:19.030
I claim her as my favorite author,

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:20.860
but she was a tremendous advocate

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:23.070
for NAMI in California

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:25.810
and started a chapter in Los Angeles

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:26.643
where she lived

00:37:26.643 --> 00:37:28.700
because she was leaving her community

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:31.070
to go to a predominantly white area

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:32.980
to get the services from NAMI.

00:37:32.980 --> 00:37:35.040
So she's now deceased,

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:36.760
but at the same time, she would say,

00:37:36.760 --> 00:37:38.100
you need to get a checkup

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:39.550
from the neck up.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:40.610
We go get our physicals.

00:37:40.610 --> 00:37:43.480
Hopefully everybody goes once a year.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:44.490
Why just stop here?

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:45.760
Like go up as well.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:47.270
You can ask your doctor

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:49.610
for a mental health survey or inventory

00:37:49.610 --> 00:37:50.820
or whatever they may call it.

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:53.150
And you can do that for
your children as well.

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:54.750
I was very, very pleased

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:57.260
with the pediatrician's office here,

00:37:57.260 --> 00:37:58.200
when my son was younger,

00:37:58.200 --> 00:37:59.550
they're both adults now.

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:01.360
And happy International Men's Day,

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:02.930
I must say,

00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:04.118
I've never heard of that before.

00:38:04.118 --> 00:38:05.218
I've heard that today.

00:38:06.285 --> 00:38:07.327
- Me neither.

00:38:07.327 --> 00:38:08.980
- I was gonna send both of my sons a text

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:11.003
and just saying, just to say hi.

00:38:12.773 --> 00:38:13.703
- That works, that works.

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:17.890
- But anyway, they have them available

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:18.850
for children as well.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:20.360
We can't say, oh, they're too young.

00:38:20.360 --> 00:38:22.410
No, we have to be proactive.

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:24.500
Don't operate out of crisis,

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:26.790
don't wait until it gets to,

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:27.980
these behaviors being

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:30.920
okay, now something has
to be addressed here.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:32.230
You don't have to get to that point,

00:38:32.230 --> 00:38:33.390
be proactive.

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:34.990
When you go for your physicals,

00:38:34.990 --> 00:38:37.510
ask for mental health inventory as well.

00:38:37.510 --> 00:38:40.120
It doesn't mean that you have concerns,

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:41.930
but if it identifies something,

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:43.280
why not treat it?

00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:44.760
Why not find out what you can do

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:46.040
to help yourself?

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:50.190
Versus waiting to like
gets to a bad, bad place,

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:51.310
which it could.

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:53.220
- Yeah, yeah, definitely agree.

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:55.440
Well, I was a neuroscientist
who studies the brain.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:56.577
I agree with both of you

00:38:56.577 --> 00:38:57.730
and I'm actually impressed

00:38:57.730 --> 00:39:00.230
that you both brought
that up before I did.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:01.810
I mean, it's critical,
I mean, to be honest,

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:03.020
I think as neuroscientists,

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:05.010
that's something we need
to do a lot better of

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:06.740
in terms of educating
the public and saying,

00:39:06.740 --> 00:39:09.100
well, there are things are
going wrong in your brain

00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:10.980
that's gonna have consequences.

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:11.870
I mean, we think about that

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:13.430
when we think about strokes

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:14.263
generally in society,

00:39:14.263 --> 00:39:15.220
we don't think about that.

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:18.250
We think about things like
depression or anxiety,

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:19.880
because there are so many components

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:21.370
that can lead to those things,

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:22.710
but we tend to forget that

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:24.370
that happens through the brain.

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:26.460
Like these experiences that we have

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:27.720
impact our brains,

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.870
which impacts how we
approach these situations.

00:39:30.870 --> 00:39:33.640
So, I mean, you're both
experts in my mind already.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:35.170
But yeah, that's
something that I've shared

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:36.160
with audiences as well too.

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:38.210
Like if I, just based
on the things we study,

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.640
if I go and I increase

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:42.550
the levels of certain brain chemicals

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:43.930
in a certain way,

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:45.380
more than likely that can push you

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:47.130
into one direction or another

00:39:47.130 --> 00:39:48.627
and lead to depression, or relate to that.

00:39:48.627 --> 00:39:49.900
So, in the same way, I mean,

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:50.940
I always give the funny example.

00:39:50.940 --> 00:39:52.440
If someone broke their arm,

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:53.580
they wouldn't just leave it

00:39:53.580 --> 00:39:55.360
and say, oh, I'm just gonna kind of

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:56.840
get some counseling from my arm

00:39:56.840 --> 00:39:57.790
or pray over my arm,

00:39:57.790 --> 00:39:58.623
and then maybe it'll get better.

00:39:58.623 --> 00:40:00.820
You go to the doctor, you get a cast.

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:03.620
But we do that with our
brains all the time.

00:40:03.620 --> 00:40:05.180
irrespective of what type of experience

00:40:05.180 --> 00:40:07.550
may have caused that change
in the brain to occur,

00:40:07.550 --> 00:40:08.860
but we sometimes just leave it

00:40:08.860 --> 00:40:09.920
and don't actually address it.

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:11.590
So I love that checkup from the neck up

00:40:11.590 --> 00:40:14.190
and all those components.

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:16.463
- Doesn't our brain
control our whole body?

00:40:17.460 --> 00:40:19.913
So we have to have it in optimum shape.

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:23.920
- And I think it is so
connected to stigma.

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:25.370
I think people don't think about it

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:26.203
as part of the brain or what,

00:40:26.203 --> 00:40:28.690
because they don't want to deal with it,

00:40:28.690 --> 00:40:30.200
they don't want it to
be happening to them,

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:31.670
it isn't real, it isn't true

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:33.320
if I just pretend to ignore it.

00:40:33.320 --> 00:40:34.550
And we know that that doesn't work,

00:40:34.550 --> 00:40:37.110
we know that that's not the case.

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:38.880
And I think when we think about stigma,

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:40.330
so often we think about,

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:42.620
creating a space where
people feel comfortable

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:44.740
coming out about what
they're going through.

00:40:44.740 --> 00:40:46.620
And I think we also really need

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.860
to create a sense of inquiry

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:52.540
where we are actually feeling empowered

00:40:52.540 --> 00:40:55.140
to say to someone who may seem like

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:57.230
things are not going so well.

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:58.700
To say, hey, are you okay?

00:40:58.700 --> 00:40:59.533
I'm worried about you.

00:40:59.533 --> 00:41:01.030
It's not enough to say,

00:41:01.030 --> 00:41:02.410
well, we just have to create a world

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:04.160
where people feel
comfortable coming forward

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:05.440
and talking about it.

00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:07.600
If you're living with
serious mental health issues,

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:09.960
you may not have the
ability to talk about it,

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:11.540
to come out and talk about it.

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:12.930
It's on the rest of us,

00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:14.110
those other four

00:41:15.380 --> 00:41:17.190
to find a way to say,

00:41:17.190 --> 00:41:19.110
I'm worried about you, are you okay?

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:20.915
How can I help?

00:41:20.915 --> 00:41:21.893
I think that's so important

00:41:21.893 --> 00:41:22.770
if we're gonna really move

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:23.910
the needle on this

00:41:23.910 --> 00:41:25.720
and kind of pull the curtain back.

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:26.610
- Yeah, yeah,

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:27.903
I definitely agree with you.

00:41:27.903 --> 00:41:29.360
I think another layer on that too,

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:31.920
is just that the fear
that comes with that too,

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.570
and maybe that's also a fear of unknown.

00:41:33.570 --> 00:41:36.170
Because when it comes to
the stigma, obviously,

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:37.690
but also our brains,

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:38.900
sometimes there's a fear of, well,

00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:41.610
what is that going to entail?

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:43.270
And medication can be effective,

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:44.960
but medication isn't the only way

00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:46.140
we change our brains.

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:47.770
So I mean, even things like meditation,

00:41:47.770 --> 00:41:48.700
change our brains,

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:50.390
being in community, changes our brains.

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:52.110
I mean, I think at this point in time,

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:53.570
a lot of us have a better feel for that,

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:54.430
being in isolation,

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:55.900
something felt different

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:57.280
and that's because of the effects

00:41:57.280 --> 00:41:58.760
that community has on our brains

00:41:58.760 --> 00:41:59.593
and how it engages

00:41:59.593 --> 00:42:00.980
certain reward pathways in our brains

00:42:00.980 --> 00:42:01.860
that help us feel better

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:04.450
and go through our daily rhythms.

00:42:04.450 --> 00:42:06.010
I mean, there's even studies looking at,

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:06.843
the power of prayer

00:42:06.843 --> 00:42:08.237
and how that can impact the brain.

00:42:08.237 --> 00:42:10.470
And counseling can impact
the brain, medication.

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:11.930
So, we have to have an approach

00:42:11.930 --> 00:42:13.670
where we take all of
these different factors

00:42:13.670 --> 00:42:14.680
and obviously I'm biased

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:15.760
and focusing on the brain.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:16.860
But as Corey mentioned,

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:18.220
the brain does control our bodies,

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:19.860
so it's legitimate bias.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:22.080
But just think about all
these different components

00:42:22.080 --> 00:42:24.760
and ways that can
actually impact our brains

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:26.160
and how we interact with each other

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:28.210
and not being afraid to engage in those.

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:29.580
So I think, in a lot of ways,

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:30.490
as I'm talking out loud,

00:42:30.490 --> 00:42:33.050
there's probably more that
we all should be doing

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:34.250
in partnerships with this.

00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:35.390
With the advocacy work,

00:42:35.390 --> 00:42:36.240
with the neuroscience,

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:38.900
to really bring these
types of conversations

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:39.733
to the forefront.

00:42:39.733 --> 00:42:41.240
So people have a better understanding

00:42:41.240 --> 00:42:43.150
and even try and move past the stigma

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:44.470
in that sense as well.

00:42:44.470 --> 00:42:45.330
So just to give a story,

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:46.943
I had given a talk years back,

00:42:46.943 --> 00:42:48.550
with the Public Library

00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:50.660
that I called The
Neuroscience of Addiction,

00:42:50.660 --> 00:42:52.160
just to kind of get a sense

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:53.230
and to help people understand

00:42:53.230 --> 00:42:55.130
what happens when you have exposure

00:42:55.130 --> 00:42:56.810
to certain substances for periods of time.

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.780
And so people from the
community came to that.

00:42:58.780 --> 00:42:59.900
There were also a bunch of folks

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:00.800
from a recovery house,

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.220
that came to that as well.

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:04.380
And so that turned into
a really nice dialogue.

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:05.530
It wasn't just me there

00:43:05.530 --> 00:43:07.280
kind of giving didactic information.

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:09.700
But I was sharing what
we knew about the brain,

00:43:09.700 --> 00:43:12.170
they were also sharing
from their experience.

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:13.920
So in a sense, we both
learn from each other.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:14.753
So a lot of the folks,

00:43:14.753 --> 00:43:16.650
some of the folks walked in and said,

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.440
oh, well, I don't really
see this as an illness

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:20.170
because I did this to myself.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:21.830
Which at some level is true,

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:23.030
you can't become addicted

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:23.863
if you've never used

00:43:23.863 --> 00:43:25.190
a substance in the first place.

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:26.890
But by the end of the session,

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:27.723
they said, okay, well,

00:43:27.723 --> 00:43:29.630
now I have a better understanding

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:32.290
of what my use has
actually done to my brain

00:43:32.290 --> 00:43:33.930
and how the brain I have now

00:43:33.930 --> 00:43:35.690
is not the same as it was before.

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:38.030
So it also gave them a sense of ownership

00:43:38.030 --> 00:43:39.280
of ways to move forward,

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:40.660
to get to a new normal,

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:42.343
without being stuck in a,

00:43:43.190 --> 00:43:44.023
this isn't an illness

00:43:44.023 --> 00:43:45.327
and there's not much I can do about it.

00:43:45.327 --> 00:43:46.250
So, I mean, I feel like

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:48.410
that was a really useful conversation

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:50.750
for both of us to be able to both sides,

00:43:50.750 --> 00:43:52.630
to be able to get to a
better place of understanding

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:54.220
and also me as a scientist,

00:43:54.220 --> 00:43:55.770
not just talking about those things

00:43:55.770 --> 00:43:58.120
and not thinking about how
people are experiencing

00:43:58.120 --> 00:43:59.380
some of the things they're going through,

00:43:59.380 --> 00:44:00.490
whether it's with a medication,

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:02.620
or medication assisted treatments

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:03.730
and all those types of things.

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:05.750
So, 'cause I'm saying we need to have

00:44:05.750 --> 00:44:07.020
more of what we're doing right here,

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:09.120
and it can really go a long way.

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:10.540
- And nutrition as well,

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:11.390
we often leave that out.

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:14.107
But the things we eat and drink,

00:44:14.107 --> 00:44:16.010
just whatever we consume,

00:44:16.010 --> 00:44:18.100
impacts our brain health

00:44:18.100 --> 00:44:19.810
and overall physical health as well.

00:44:19.810 --> 00:44:21.960
So we can make a lot of changes

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:24.500
just by being aware

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:26.563
of what we are putting into our mouths.

00:44:29.330 --> 00:44:30.820
- And it's so empowering too,

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:33.140
to recognize that there are things over

00:44:33.140 --> 00:44:35.240
which I actually have some control,

00:44:35.240 --> 00:44:37.620
this isn't happening to me

00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:38.500
in every way,

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:40.387
I can control my response to this.

00:44:40.387 --> 00:44:42.330
And I think, well,

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:43.900
we have a group called Hearing Voices,

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:44.733
for voice hears,

00:44:44.733 --> 00:44:46.290
people who hear voices

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:48.300
and some of them are on medication,

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:50.480
some of them are in
counseling and treatment,

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:51.560
some are doing both.

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:52.650
And some have said,

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:55.110
I don't want to take medication,

00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:57.320
I can tolerate the voices,

00:44:57.320 --> 00:44:58.500
it's manageable,

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:00.100
it's not having a negative impact

00:45:00.100 --> 00:45:01.490
on my life right now,

00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:02.930
and I'm okay with that.

00:45:02.930 --> 00:45:05.490
So there's a level of autonomy

00:45:06.350 --> 00:45:08.160
that people actually can have,

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:10.330
an agency and power

00:45:10.330 --> 00:45:12.130
in how they're going to approach this.

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:13.630
There's not, again, it's not that

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:17.170
one size fits all
approach to getting well,

00:45:17.170 --> 00:45:18.330
to doing better.

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:19.840
You have to pay attention

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.520
to what's going on for you

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:23.270
and really figure out what those,

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:25.450
to the extent you can figure out

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:26.730
what those pieces are

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:30.280
that are your holistic
approach to treatment.

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:32.270
Even through the lens of self care,

00:45:32.270 --> 00:45:33.580
I feel like self-care sometimes

00:45:33.580 --> 00:45:34.950
can feel so prescribed,

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:37.720
like it's meditating, yoga, or running.

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:40.150
Well, if you don't do any
of those three things,

00:45:40.150 --> 00:45:42.390
you beat yourself up for not doing it

00:45:42.390 --> 00:45:44.880
and then you're in this negative cycle

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:45.900
as opposed to self care,

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:47.520
you're doing self harm.

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:49.702
So like Corey was saying,

00:45:49.702 --> 00:45:51.160
we tried to think about self-care as,

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:53.180
what do you need right now to be okay?

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:55.240
And maybe it is just sitting by the water,

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.860
and maybe it is yoga,
meditation or running,

00:45:57.860 --> 00:45:59.250
maybe it's just crying

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:00.310
or looking out the window

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:02.810
at the leaves falling off of the trees.

00:46:02.810 --> 00:46:04.393
It's excuse me.

00:46:05.360 --> 00:46:09.612
We can play a part in moving that needle

00:46:09.612 --> 00:46:11.670
and where we are by using the resources,

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:13.960
the tools, the supports that we have

00:46:13.960 --> 00:46:16.070
to create that kind of wellness

00:46:16.070 --> 00:46:18.110
approach for ourselves.

00:46:18.110 --> 00:46:20.610
- Yeah, I definitely agree,

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:21.443
definitely awesome.

00:46:21.443 --> 00:46:22.460
And I loved the examples too,

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:25.060
of just meeting people where they are.

00:46:25.060 --> 00:46:25.940
Even as you're talking about,

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:27.590
some can tolerate the voices,

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:28.610
some don't want the voice

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:29.850
and just saying, well, what,

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:31.840
it goes back to a point
Corey is making as well.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:33.070
I think Matt you're also making it.

00:46:33.070 --> 00:46:34.570
Like, what do you need

00:46:34.570 --> 00:46:36.960
to actually navigate the situation?

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:38.080
And that's a topic that came up

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:39.810
with the last case as well, Sean Astin

00:46:39.810 --> 00:46:41.410
talking about his mom, Patty Duke,

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:42.780
and some of the challenges

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:44.090
with the language that was used

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:46.340
in terms of suffering versus navigating

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:48.130
and the fact that we can navigate

00:46:48.130 --> 00:46:50.790
through these things
and still be at a place

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:53.920
of having overall health and

00:46:56.310 --> 00:46:59.030
not great, kind of wellness in our lives,

00:46:59.030 --> 00:47:00.660
even if there are some struggles

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:01.990
that are going on in the background.

00:47:01.990 --> 00:47:02.920
And so, I mean, Matt,

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:03.940
you talked about the spectrum,

00:47:03.940 --> 00:47:05.490
Corey, you talked about the spectrum.

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:08.290
Even if someone has a serious
mental health challenge,

00:47:08.290 --> 00:47:11.220
but has navigated in a way
that's still effective.

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:12.180
We can't ignore that

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:14.130
and we can't put that in a box of,

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:15.890
oh, this is still bad.

00:47:15.890 --> 00:47:16.950
Or someone who was on the,

00:47:16.950 --> 00:47:18.450
we have to really just have

00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:19.607
a broader perspective on these things

00:47:19.607 --> 00:47:20.740
and help people navigate

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:22.500
and thrive in a lot of ways.

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:25.000
- And we don't hear those stories enough.

00:47:25.000 --> 00:47:26.420
We hear so many stories

00:47:26.420 --> 00:47:28.110
of people living with mental illness

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:29.380
who are not doing well,

00:47:29.380 --> 00:47:31.620
or people who are committing crimes

00:47:31.620 --> 00:47:35.580
or doing like wildly inappropriate,

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:37.400
awful things who we assume

00:47:37.400 --> 00:47:39.580
are living with mental illness.

00:47:39.580 --> 00:47:40.790
But we don't really hear stories

00:47:40.790 --> 00:47:42.630
of people living with
serious mental illness

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:44.490
who are doing all right,

00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:45.360
they're doing well.

00:47:45.360 --> 00:47:46.340
There's so many ideas,

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:48.440
so many misconceptions around what,

00:47:48.440 --> 00:47:49.530
who lives with mental illness

00:47:49.530 --> 00:47:50.970
and what it means if you do.

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:54.620
And I think to normalize
the reality of it,

00:47:54.620 --> 00:47:56.670
that recovery is real,

00:47:56.670 --> 00:47:58.980
that you can live a rich, full life

00:47:58.980 --> 00:48:01.070
with a diagnosis of mental illness,

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:02.343
serious mental illness.

00:48:03.660 --> 00:48:05.290
We need to tell more of those stories

00:48:05.290 --> 00:48:08.410
about people who are living well

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:10.500
and not focusing on sort of the negative

00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:12.760
or assumed attributes

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:14.790
of who's living with mental illness.

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:15.623
- That's really well said.

00:48:15.623 --> 00:48:16.700
And those assumptions can lead

00:48:16.700 --> 00:48:17.700
to all sorts of,

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:19.540
often those aren't even correct.

00:48:19.540 --> 00:48:21.730
And the ones that are
the minority of the cases

00:48:21.730 --> 00:48:22.970
get blown up

00:48:22.970 --> 00:48:25.260
and get the focus and the attention.

00:48:25.260 --> 00:48:26.093
So I think it's really interesting.

00:48:26.093 --> 00:48:28.730
- And just perpetuate the misunderstanding

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:31.997
and that contributes to the stigma

00:48:31.997 --> 00:48:33.100
and that keeps people

00:48:33.100 --> 00:48:35.890
who are living with these
issues in the shadows.

00:48:35.890 --> 00:48:37.690
So it's just this negative cycle.

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:38.923
- Yeah, I mean, especially
like with the violence

00:48:38.923 --> 00:48:40.160
that you talked about,

00:48:40.160 --> 00:48:41.320
because the pervasive thought

00:48:41.320 --> 00:48:42.510
is that people with mental illness

00:48:42.510 --> 00:48:45.930
are more likely to commit
those types of crimes,

00:48:45.930 --> 00:48:48.130
which is completely non-factual and false

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:50.170
because they're much
more likely to be victims

00:48:50.170 --> 00:48:51.003
than to be the ones.

00:48:51.003 --> 00:48:52.357
But if you talk to people
in the general public

00:48:52.357 --> 00:48:56.090
has not the current
understanding of things,

00:48:56.090 --> 00:48:57.270
even though it's completely incorrect.

00:48:57.270 --> 00:48:59.100
- No, not at all.

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:02.183
And I think even further to that point,

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:04.430
we're assuming that,

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:06.820
like clearly someone who's committing

00:49:06.820 --> 00:49:08.670
those kinds of acts of violence

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:09.860
are not doing well,

00:49:09.860 --> 00:49:11.680
there's something going on for them.

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:14.760
But to just sort of label
them as mentally ill

00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:17.320
is such a gross injustice,

00:49:17.320 --> 00:49:18.430
it's not fair.

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:20.080
It's not fair to them,

00:49:20.080 --> 00:49:23.190
it just shines the wrong light

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:25.290
on what these issues are

00:49:25.290 --> 00:49:26.683
and who's living with them.

00:49:27.620 --> 00:49:28.983
- Yeah, so as we wrap up,

00:49:28.983 --> 00:49:32.010
I think we're also
touching on the many things

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.760
that we still have to do as a society.

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:35.180
So I'm curious, from both of you,

00:49:35.180 --> 00:49:36.690
obviously the work that you're doing

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:38.670
is so important, is making a huge impact.

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:41.670
But what do you see as the next steps

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:42.580
that we need to make

00:49:42.580 --> 00:49:44.420
either within your organizations

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:45.253
and your initiatives

00:49:45.253 --> 00:49:46.963
or as a society in general?

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:49.520
Not a small question.

00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:50.880
- Yes, not a small question,

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:52.540
and there is not a small answer.

00:49:52.540 --> 00:49:54.500
But like we need an overhaul

00:49:54.500 --> 00:49:56.850
to the mental health system.

00:49:56.850 --> 00:49:59.220
It's too difficult to get help.

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:00.990
And I feel for those families

00:50:00.990 --> 00:50:02.390
that don't have help

00:50:02.390 --> 00:50:03.980
or don't know where to go for help,

00:50:03.980 --> 00:50:05.520
which I'm trying to use my voice

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:06.800
as much as possible.

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:08.410
But also housing,

00:50:08.410 --> 00:50:09.900
housing is one of the first things

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:11.160
that a person loses,

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:14.240
dealing with severe mental
illness, as an example,

00:50:14.240 --> 00:50:15.517
because of the behaviors.

00:50:15.517 --> 00:50:17.040
And they may be interpreted

00:50:17.040 --> 00:50:20.730
as violating lease terms for example.

00:50:20.730 --> 00:50:24.250
But having housing
available, quality housing,

00:50:24.250 --> 00:50:26.370
I've seen some deplorable conditions

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:28.670
in group homes and things of that nature,

00:50:28.670 --> 00:50:30.950
but they still get the full dollar

00:50:30.950 --> 00:50:33.100
for an individual staying there.

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:35.080
So some of the things that I wanna work on

00:50:35.080 --> 00:50:37.940
is just helping with legislation

00:50:37.940 --> 00:50:40.680
that can help increase the quality of life

00:50:40.680 --> 00:50:43.350
for those living with
sever mental illness,

00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:45.520
as well as quality housing,

00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:47.840
being available with the understanding

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:51.110
of behaviors and how they can just

00:50:52.010 --> 00:50:55.580
have the opportunity to live in quality,

00:50:55.580 --> 00:50:57.743
I can't stress that
enough quality housing,

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:02.140
with property owners that understand

00:51:02.140 --> 00:51:04.550
the behaviors that may be exhibited

00:51:04.550 --> 00:51:06.660
at any given time.

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:07.873
- Yeah, so well said.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:10.790
- And NAMI NYC.

00:51:10.790 --> 00:51:12.050
We're really focused on reaching

00:51:12.050 --> 00:51:14.170
underserved communities right now.

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:16.740
And we're defining that
in pretty broad ways.

00:51:16.740 --> 00:51:19.280
So, black and brown communities of course,

00:51:19.280 --> 00:51:21.520
also deaf and hard of hearing communities,

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:24.320
the LGBTQ communities, older adults,

00:51:24.320 --> 00:51:26.100
non native English speakers.

00:51:26.100 --> 00:51:27.730
We really wanna make sure that,

00:51:27.730 --> 00:51:28.970
the programs and services

00:51:28.970 --> 00:51:30.160
that we're offering,

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.370
not only do all individuals

00:51:32.370 --> 00:51:34.080
understand what they are

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:35.040
that they're free

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:36.550
and how easy it is to access,

00:51:36.550 --> 00:51:37.870
but that they, again,

00:51:37.870 --> 00:51:39.720
they feel seen when they show up,

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:41.460
when they come and they dip their toe

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:44.760
in the NAMI NYC water, so to speak.

00:51:44.760 --> 00:51:47.560
And then we're also focusing
on the family piece,

00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:49.790
the family caregiver piece.

00:51:49.790 --> 00:51:51.940
We're continuing to provide programs

00:51:51.940 --> 00:51:53.307
for people who are living
with mental illness

00:51:53.307 --> 00:51:55.020
and we always will.

00:51:55.020 --> 00:51:56.950
But one of the things we recognize is,

00:51:56.950 --> 00:51:58.930
we're really the only resource

00:51:58.930 --> 00:51:59.850
for family members,

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:02.310
especially family members of adults.

00:52:02.310 --> 00:52:03.970
And so we wanna make sure

00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:07.150
that families know that there's knowledge

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:10.630
and skill and support that you can learn

00:52:10.630 --> 00:52:12.090
from our 40 year history

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:13.960
that can make a meaningful difference,

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:15.280
not only in your life

00:52:15.280 --> 00:52:16.830
and in the life of the individual

00:52:16.830 --> 00:52:18.410
who you care so much about,

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:21.010
but in your shared life.

00:52:21.010 --> 00:52:22.477
So often we're winging it

00:52:22.477 --> 00:52:23.470
and when we're winging it,

00:52:23.470 --> 00:52:24.980
there's a tremendous amount of tension

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:27.160
and friction that tears families apart.

00:52:27.160 --> 00:52:28.480
But it can be better,

00:52:28.480 --> 00:52:29.890
it can be when we know

00:52:29.890 --> 00:52:31.130
what to say, what not to say,

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:31.963
when to say,

00:52:31.963 --> 00:52:32.872
and when not to say it,

00:52:32.872 --> 00:52:36.150
how to say it, excuse me.

00:52:36.150 --> 00:52:39.010
To separate the person
from their diagnosis

00:52:39.010 --> 00:52:41.222
and their symptoms and see them separately

00:52:41.222 --> 00:52:43.260
so that I can hate bipolar disorder,

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:45.800
but I love this person.

00:52:45.800 --> 00:52:46.730
We're really focusing

00:52:46.730 --> 00:52:49.220
on those family pieces as well.

00:52:49.220 --> 00:52:51.530
And then as Corey mentioned, advocacy.

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:53.297
We can have all the programs
and services in the world,

00:52:53.297 --> 00:52:55.800
but if we don't improve
the mental health system

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:56.680
and for us specific,

00:52:56.680 --> 00:52:58.010
we're working on five tenants,

00:52:58.010 --> 00:52:59.720
but the one I'll call out today

00:52:59.720 --> 00:53:02.170
is decriminalizing mental illness.

00:53:02.170 --> 00:53:03.840
It's so critically important

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:05.580
for us to really move the system,

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:07.350
to change the mental health system

00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:10.120
so that it really can be
mental health for all.

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:13.433
- Yes, well said.

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:16.410
- Well, that wasn't an easy question,

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:17.703
but I definitely appreciate
both of the answers

00:53:17.703 --> 00:53:18.657
that you both mentioned.

00:53:18.657 --> 00:53:20.460
And I encourage listeners too,

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:22.840
to just reach out to
your local NAMI as well,

00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:24.400
if there are things,
resources you're looking for

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:26.630
or ways get involved and to support.

00:53:26.630 --> 00:53:27.503
And Corey, even as you mentioned,

00:53:27.503 --> 00:53:28.700
with the housing.

00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:30.270
Any specific suggestions,

00:53:30.270 --> 00:53:32.040
Corey for listeners in terms of,

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:33.450
ways that they may be able to get involved

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:36.940
in some of these fights
from a legal perspective.

00:53:36.940 --> 00:53:39.310
- Yes, contacting their
local representatives,

00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:40.310
state representatives,

00:53:40.310 --> 00:53:42.810
as well as their federal legislators

00:53:42.810 --> 00:53:45.150
and finding out NAMI is very good,

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:48.270
it has an advocacy piece on their website

00:53:48.270 --> 00:53:52.020
where it tells you about
pending legislation

00:53:52.020 --> 00:53:55.590
and how to communicate
with your Congress member.

00:53:55.590 --> 00:53:58.580
And it's just important for us to know

00:53:58.580 --> 00:54:00.350
that these things are important.

00:54:00.350 --> 00:54:02.700
The emails, the phone call,

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:03.650
they are important.

00:54:03.650 --> 00:54:05.650
So we may not think
we're making a big impact

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:07.340
because it's just one phone call from us,

00:54:07.340 --> 00:54:09.270
but you can get your family and friends

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:10.103
to do it as well.

00:54:10.103 --> 00:54:11.610
And if there's ever a hearing

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:13.900
that you can go and sit in

00:54:13.900 --> 00:54:15.920
and even be able to speak,

00:54:15.920 --> 00:54:17.990
I suggest you please let people know

00:54:17.990 --> 00:54:20.850
that you are willing to do that and share

00:54:20.850 --> 00:54:24.870
how important some of these
pieces of legislation are.

00:54:24.870 --> 00:54:27.300
Especially with the exclusion,

00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:28.680
I'm sure you can give me the,

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:32.060
is it the IMD exclusion for the beds?

00:54:32.060 --> 00:54:33.920
There's like a limit on the number of beds

00:54:33.920 --> 00:54:36.820
that are available in order for a refund

00:54:36.820 --> 00:54:38.560
to occur to that local entity.

00:54:38.560 --> 00:54:40.960
And so, it's just unfortunate

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:42.770
that it's all about money

00:54:42.770 --> 00:54:44.760
when you have real lives

00:54:44.760 --> 00:54:48.060
that are being impacted
and need the services.

00:54:48.060 --> 00:54:50.010
So, I just encourage individuals

00:54:50.010 --> 00:54:51.780
to go to organization's websites,

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:54.020
like NAMI, like Mental Health America,

00:54:54.020 --> 00:54:56.150
like the Treatment Advocacy Center

00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:58.460
and look at their legislation

00:54:58.460 --> 00:55:01.190
and policy and procedure piece

00:55:01.190 --> 00:55:04.570
because they have so many
things already in place,

00:55:04.570 --> 00:55:05.950
templates you can use,

00:55:05.950 --> 00:55:07.850
they help you to be able to communicate

00:55:07.850 --> 00:55:10.150
with legislators who can make a difference

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:13.790
and the time is now to
make that difference.

00:55:13.790 --> 00:55:15.180
- That's great advice.

